WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.065
A practical threat hunting workflow starts with a defensive question. What behavior are

00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:04.780
you looking for,

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.550
and why does it matter to the environment?

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.080
The question should be narrow enough to investigate and broad enough to produce useful

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:10.580
learning.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.800
From there, define scope: business unit, asset group, identity set, cloud account,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.320
time window, and data sources.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.880
Scope keeps the work bounded and makes conclusions more honest. Next, write a hypothesis.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.990
A good hypothesis names suspected behavior, affected assets or identities,

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.745
required telemetry, expected observations, and decision criteria.

00:00:28.745 --> 00:00:31.210
It should be testable. It should also be allowed to fail.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.240
A rejected hypothesis can still be valuable if it was tested carefully and documented

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.205
clearly. Then identify telemetry.

00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:41.090
Decide which endpoint, identity, DNS, proxy, firewall, EDR, cloud,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.140
or email data should show the behavior.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.845
Check retention, field quality, timestamps, host naming, and known gaps.

00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:48.480
If the data cannot answer the question,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.060
document that limitation and refine the hunt. Query and pivot deliberately.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.060
Start with the behavior, not a random search.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.050
Pivot from host to user, user to source IP, process to parent process,

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.250
domain to DNS clients, hash to endpoints,

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.085
or alert to surrounding timeline when the pivot helps test the hypothesis.

00:01:04.085 --> 00:01:08.325
Reduce noise, but do not filter so aggressively that the signal disappears. Save queries,

00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:10.565
assumptions, exclusions, and time windows.

00:01:10.565 --> 00:01:14.805
Validate before concluding. Compare against baselines, asset roles, user context,

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:17.130
business explanations, and related events.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.675
Use careful language: unusual, suspicious, malicious, and confirmed are not the same.

00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:25.675
Escalate to incident response when the evidence meets your organization's criteria,

00:01:25.675 --> 00:01:28.085
and preserve the evidence needed for responders to act.

00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:30.555
Finally, document the outcome and improve defenses.

00:01:30.555 --> 00:01:35.620
A hunt can produce new detection logic, tuned alerts, improved logging, updated playbooks,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.280
documented gaps, asset context, or threat intelligence feedback.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.840
Threat hunting is not random searching or alert chasing.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.900
It is structured investigation that turns questions into evidence and evidence into

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.520
stronger security operations.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.670
For a beginner, the checklist is intentionally simple. Question, scope, hypothesis,

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.685
telemetry, query, pivot, validate, document, improve.

00:01:54.685 --> 00:01:55.875
If a hunt feels confusing,

00:01:55.875 --> 00:01:59.180
return to that sequence. The workflow does not remove analyst judgment,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.720
but it gives judgment a structure.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.460
Over time,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:06.080
that structure helps a SOC turn individual curiosity into repeatable defensive capability.
